.SH NAME
frontend/node_modules/delayed-stream Directory Reference
.SH SYNOPSIS
.br
.PP
.SH "Detailed Description"
.PP 

.SH "delayed-stream"
.PP
Buffers events from a stream until you are ready to handle them\&.
.SH "Installation"
.PP
.PP
.nf
 bash
npm install delayed\-stream
.fi
.PP
.SH "Usage"
.PP
The following example shows how to write a http echo server that delays its response by 1000 ms\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
 javascript
var DelayedStream = require('delayed\-stream');
var http = require('http');

http\&.createServer(function(req, res) {
  var delayed = DelayedStream\&.create(req);

  setTimeout(function() {
    res\&.writeHead(200);
    delayed\&.pipe(res);
  }, 1000);
});
.fi
.PP

.PP
If you are not using \fRStream#pipe\fP, you can also manually release the buffered events by calling \fRdelayedStream\&.resume()\fP:

.PP
.PP
.nf
 javascript
var delayed = DelayedStream\&.create(req);

setTimeout(function() {
  // Emit all buffered events and resume underlaying source
  delayed\&.resume();
}, 1000);
.fi
.PP
.SH "Implementation"
.PP
In order to use this meta stream properly, here are a few things you should know about the implementation\&.
.SS "Event Buffering / Proxying"
All events of the \fRsource\fP stream are hijacked by overwriting the \fRsource\&.emit\fP method\&. Until node implements a catch-all event listener, this is the only way\&.

.PP
However, delayed-stream still continues to emit all events it captures on the \fRsource\fP, regardless of whether you have released the delayed stream yet or not\&.

.PP
Upon creation, delayed-stream captures all \fRsource\fP events and stores them in an internal event buffer\&. Once \fRdelayedStream\&.release()\fP is called, all buffered events are emitted on the \fRdelayedStream\fP, and the event buffer is cleared\&. After that, delayed-stream merely acts as a proxy for the underlaying source\&.
.SS "Error handling"
Error events on \fRsource\fP are buffered / proxied just like any other events\&. However, \fRdelayedStream\&.create\fP attaches a no-op \fR'error'\fP listener to the \fRsource\fP\&. This way you only have to handle errors on the \fRdelayedStream\fP object, rather than in two places\&.
.SS "Buffer limits"
delayed-stream provides a \fRmaxDataSize\fP property that can be used to limit the amount of data being buffered\&. In order to protect you from bad \fRsource\fP streams that don't react to \fRsource\&.pause()\fP, this feature is enabled by default\&.
.SH "API"
.PP
.SS "DelayedStream\&.create(source, [options])"
Returns a new \fRdelayedStream\fP\&. Available options are:

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRpauseStream\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRmaxDataSize\fP
.PP

.PP
The description for those properties can be found below\&.
.SS "delayedStream\&.source"
The \fRsource\fP stream managed by this object\&. This is useful if you are passing your \fRdelayedStream\fP around, and you still want to access properties on the \fRsource\fP object\&.
.SS "delayedStream\&.pauseStream = true"
Whether to pause the underlaying \fRsource\fP when calling \fRDelayedStream\&.create()\fP\&. Modifying this property afterwards has no effect\&.
.SS "delayedStream\&.maxDataSize = 1024 * 1024"
The amount of data to buffer before emitting an \fRerror\fP\&.

.PP
If the underlaying source is emitting \fRBuffer\fP objects, the \fRmaxDataSize\fP refers to bytes\&.

.PP
If the underlaying source is emitting JavaScript strings, the size refers to characters\&.

.PP
If you know what you are doing, you can set this property to \fRInfinity\fP to disable this feature\&. You can also modify this property during runtime\&.
.SS "delayedStream\&.dataSize = 0"
The amount of data buffered so far\&.
.SS "delayedStream\&.readable"
An ECMA5 getter that returns the value of \fRsource\&.readable\fP\&.
.SS "delayedStream\&.resume()"
If the \fRdelayedStream\fP has not been released so far, \fRdelayedStream\&.release()\fP is called\&.

.PP
In either case, \fRsource\&.resume()\fP is called\&.
.SS "delayedStream\&.pause()"
Calls \fRsource\&.pause()\fP\&.
.SS "delayedStream\&.pipe(dest)"
Calls \fRdelayedStream\&.resume()\fP and then proxies the arguments to \fRsource\&.pipe\fP\&.
.SS "delayedStream\&.release()"
Emits and clears all events that have been buffered up so far\&. This does not resume the underlaying source, use \fRdelayedStream\&.resume()\fP instead\&.
